            </nav>
            <p class="eyebrow">Single-Cell Data</p>
            <h1>Bridging Machine Learning and Single-Cell Genomics</h1>
            <div class="article-byline">
              <span>Lydia Moreno</span>
              <span><time datetime="2025-01-01">January 2025</time></span>
              <span>5 min read</span>
            </div>
          </header>

          <div class="article-body">
            <p>
              Modern single-cell experiments rarely produce one clean matrix. A study may combine
              gene expression, chromatin accessibility, spatial measurements, and imaging from the
              same tissue. Each modality has its own noise model, missingness pattern, and scale.
              Machine learning can help integrate these views, but only when the surrounding
              pipeline is designed to preserve the structure that makes the data scientifically
              meaningful.
            </p>

            <h2>The integration problem is also a data-engineering problem</h2>
            <p>
              Before choosing a clustering algorithm or embedding method, we need a reproducible
              path from raw reads to a joint representation. That path should make every filtering
              decision explicit, retain sample and batch metadata, and allow an analysis to be
              rerun without hidden manual steps. Workflow systems such as Nextflow and Snakemake
              are valuable not because they make the statistics better, but because they make the
              chain of evidence inspectable.
            </p>

            <h3>A practical pipeline shape</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Validate input files and record checksums.</li>
              <li>Normalize and quality-control each modality independently.</li>
              <li>Preserve donor, sample, and spatial coordinates through integration.</li>
              <li>Generate a joint embedding with a documented model and random seed.</li>
              <li>Evaluate clusters against held-out markers and biological annotations.</li>
              <li>Export intermediate artifacts for downstream review.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Uncertainty belongs in the result</h2>
            <p>
              In single-cell analysis, a hard cell-type label can hide substantial uncertainty.
              Bayesian clustering and probabilistic embeddings offer a way to carry that
              uncertainty forward. A cluster that is stable across random seeds and donors is a
              stronger claim than one that appears only under a particular resolution parameter.
              The goal is not to eliminate subjective choices, but to expose them and test how
              sensitive the conclusion is to those choices.
            </p>

            <h2>Reproducibility as a scientific feature</h2>
            <p>
              The most useful computational biology tools are not only accurate; they are easy to
              audit. Versioned workflows, containerized environments, and clear parameters let
              collaborators verify what happened. They also make it easier to reuse a pipeline
              on a new tissue, species, or modality without silently carrying over assumptions.
            </p>

            <p>
              Bridging machine learning and single-cell genomics is ultimately a matter of
              connecting models to measurement. The best integrations keep the underlying
              biology visible, quantify uncertainty, and make each processing decision
              reproducible.
            </p>

            <p class="article-note">
              This post reflects my own views on research practice and is not a report of a
              specific study. For related peer-reviewed work, see
              <a href="../publications.html">the publications page</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
